# Alembic — Database Migrations
This folder contains Alembic configuration and migration scripts used to manage the database schema over time.

## Purpose
- Alembic provides a versioned migration system for SQLAlchemy projects. It lets you track schema changes (create/alter/drop tables, columns, constraints) in source control and apply them reliably across environments (development, CI, staging, production).

## Why use Alembic
- Keep schema changes explicit, reviewable and reversible.
- Ensure the same changes are applied in CI and production.
- Support rolling back changes when necessary (with caution).

## Common workflow & commands
- Create a new revision (autogenerate from models):
	```bash
	alembic revision --autogenerate -m "describe change"
	```
	After autogenerate, always review and edit the generated migration file before committing.

- Apply migrations (upgrade to latest):
	```bash
	alembic upgrade head
	```

- Rollback (downgrade one revision):
	```bash
	alembic downgrade -1
	```

- Inspect history and current head:
	```bash
	alembic history --verbose
	alembic current
	```

## Integration notes
- `env.py` configures Alembic's runtime and imports the application's SQLAlchemy metadata. For `--autogenerate` to detect model changes, ensure the ORM models are importable when Alembic runs (i.e., correct Python path / package imports in `env.py`).
- If your models live under `app/shared/db/models`, make sure those modules are imported (directly or indirectly) inside `env.py` so the metadata is available to Alembic.

## Best practices and caveats
- Autogenerate is a convenience — always review the diff. Some operations (e.g., renamed columns, complex type changes) require manual migration edits.
- Keep migration files focused and small. Use descriptive revision messages.
- Avoid destructive operations in production without backups and a rollback plan.

## CI / Deployment
- Include `alembic upgrade head` in your deployment or migration job so the DB schema is ready before the app starts. For zero-downtime, coordinate schema changes with deploy strategies.

## Troubleshooting
- If autogenerate does not pick up a model, confirm the model module is imported in `env.py` and that SQLAlchemy `MetaData` is attached to the model(s).
- If you get multiple heads, create a merge migration or coordinate with the team to consolidate changes.

## See also
- `alembic/versions/` — existing migration files for examples in this project.